"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 11.7 and the pathological T3a stage with extraprostatic extension documented in the prostatectomy specimen. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. A significant limitation is that no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status cannot be assessed, which widens uncertainty. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows concordance regarding Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1), although the second biopsy noted perineural invasion, while the surgical report did not specify this finding. Future prognosis assessment would benefit significantly from postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory to refine risk stratification. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.11–93.68 months; its coverage has not been validated."
